{# ONE site block per HOST (not per worker) -- every relay/signaling/bot/
   cp-daemon/validator-daemon instance colocated on this host shares the SAME
   public hostname (`<ip-dashed>.sslip.io`, one Let's Encrypt cert) and is
   disambiguated by a URL PATH PREFIX instead: `/<instance-name>/<worker-name>/*`,
   where instance-name = "<provider>-<host>" (e.g. "akamai-001") and
   worker-name = "<service>-<index>" (e.g. "relay-1") -- i.e. worker_key split
   at the service boundary. This MUST exactly match what deploy_one_service.yml
   injects as RELAY_ENDPOINT_URL/ENDPOINT_URL (wss://<ip-dashed>.sslip.io/
   <instance-name>/<worker-name>) or the routing here won't match what the
   client connects to. bot/cp-daemon/validator-daemon have no such env var --
   their path is whatever sslip.io address is reached directly (bot's control
   API; cp-daemon/validator-daemon's metrics-only endpoint).

   Switched from one-hostname-per-worker (N Let's Encrypt certs per host) to
   this scheme because N repeated redeploys of a colocated host were burning
   Let's Encrypt's "5 duplicate certificates per exact identifier set per
   168h" rate limit N times as fast as necessary -- one cert per HOST fixes
   that regardless of how many workers share it.

   Each worker gets a named matcher (`@<service><index>`) matching BOTH the
   bare `/<instance>/<worker>` prefix AND `/<instance>/<worker>/*` -- a glob
   pattern alone (`/<prefix>/*`) matches `/<prefix>/` and anything under it
   but NOT the bare prefix with no trailing slash and nothing after it, and
   `handle_path` only accepts a single literal path pattern (confirmed via
   `caddy validate`: passing two space-separated patterns errors with "wrong
   argument count"), so a bare `handle_path /<prefix>/*` alone silently
   404s exactly the bare-prefix case. That case is NOT hypothetical:
   confirmed live, the bot's WS client connects to exactly
   RELAY_ENDPOINT_URL (no trailing slash -- see worker_env.yml's
   xaisen_sslip_host), so the primary WS connection hit this 404 while every
   OTHER path (which always carries its own suffix -- /healthz, /metrics,
   /api/probe, etc.) matched fine and masked the bug. The `path` matcher
   (unlike `handle_path`) DOES accept multiple space-separated patterns, so
   the two-pattern matcher is built there instead; `handle` (not
   `handle_path`) then dispatches on it, and `uri strip_prefix` explicitly
   strips the prefix as the block's first directive -- functionally
   equivalent to `handle_path`, but decoupled from its single-pattern
   limit. Every INNER handle block below is otherwise BYTE-IDENTICAL to the
   old per-worker-hostname scheme -- the backend app (relay's
   metrics-server.ts, packages/shared/healthz.ts, etc.) still sees bare
   `/metrics`, `/healthz`, `/stats/*`, `/logs/*`, `/relay-down-hint*` exactly
   as before; it has no idea a prefix was ever there. relay's own WS upgrade
   handler (signaling/index.ts's WebSocketServer) has no `path` option, so it
   already accepts an upgrade on any path -- no relay-side change needed for
   the main WS route either.

   The main upstream target is each app's FIXED internal listen port
   (WS_PORT for relay, SIGNALING_PORT for signaling, PORT for bot -- see
   apps/relay/src/index.ts, apps/signaling/src/index.ts, apps/bot/src/config.ts,
   none read an env var keyed off worker_index), NOT svc.port -- that field is
   the per-worker_index-offset HOST-published port (cli/infra.py
   set_vm_defaults), which only matters for the docker_container ports:
   mapping. Caddy talks to the sibling container directly over the shared
   xaisen-net bridge, bypassing published host ports entirely, so every
   same-service replica on this host listens internally on the SAME fixed
   port and needs the SAME upstream target. cp-daemon/validator-daemon have NO
   such main surface at all (chain-facing daemons, not client-facing servers)
   -- they're absent from xaisen_internal_ports entirely, so their handle_path
   block below only gets the metrics/healthz handles, no generic fallback.

   relay/signaling/bot/cp-daemon/validator-daemon each additionally run a
   SEPARATE metrics HTTP server on its own port (METRICS_PORT=4001,
   SIGNALING_METRICS_PORT=8083, BOT_METRICS_PORT=8096, CP_METRICS_PORT=8092,
   VALIDATOR_METRICS_PORT=8103 -- a distinct Node process listener, not the
   same server as the main port above) which has no published host port and
   no firewall rule of its own (see IaC/pulumi/app/compute/*.py's per-svc.port
   inbound rules) -- it's reachable ONLY by path-routing a slice of this SAME
   public hostname to that internal port, which is what xaisen_metrics_ports +
   the handle blocks below do. Prometheus's scrape targets go through this
   same public :443 hostname (see prometheus.yml.j2's metrics_path:).
   /healthz* is NOT always on the same port as /metrics* -- only relay unifies
   both onto its metrics port (its own metrics-server.ts serves /healthz
   alongside /metrics, see RO-020). signaling/cp-daemon/validator-daemon each
   run startHealthzServer (see packages/shared/src/healthz.ts) as a SEPARATE
   node:http listener on its own *_HEALTHZ_PORT, distinct from its
   *_METRICS_PORT prom-metrics listener (which 404s on anything but
   /metrics) -- xaisen_healthz_ports below is that real per-service port, NOT
   a duplicate of xaisen_metrics_ports. bot has no healthz server at all, so
   it's absent from xaisen_healthz_ports and gets no /healthz* handle. This is
   what lets cp-daemon's relay-assignment liveness probe (room-assignment.ts)
   reach ANY candidate relay's health endpoint over the public hostname,
   regardless of whether cp-daemon happens to be colocated with that relay or
   on a different host entirely. /stats/* (the client's POST /stats/report),
   /logs/* (POST /logs/report), and /relay-down-hint* are relay's own
   app-protocol routes, not a generic metrics-server feature -- scoped to
   xaisen_stats_report_services so they aren't wrongly added for
   bot/cp-daemon/validator-daemon, which don't implement them. Without an
   explicit handle block here, a path falls through to the generic handle{}
   below and gets proxied to the WRONG port (the main WS/signaling port, not
   the metrics port these routes actually live on). #}
{% set xaisen_internal_ports = {'relay': 4000, 'bot': 8095} %}
{% set xaisen_metrics_ports = {'relay': 4001, 'bot': 8096, 'cp-daemon': 8092, 'validator-daemon': 8103} %}
{% set xaisen_healthz_ports = {'relay': 4001, 'cp-daemon': 8091, 'validator-daemon': 8101} %}
{% set xaisen_stats_report_services = ['relay'] %}
{# S30.C TURN delivery: cp-daemon's bearer-gated POST /turn/issue RPC
   (turn-rpc.ts, port from TURN_RPC_PORT, default 8090) -- relay's
   turn-fetcher.ts calls this over the public sslip.io URL
   (turn_rpc_lookup.yml's xaisen_cp_daemon_rpc_url), so it needs the same
   handle-block treatment as /metrics* and /healthz* above. #}
{% set xaisen_turn_rpc_ports = {'cp-daemon': 8090} %}
{{ ansible_host | replace('.', '-') }}.sslip.io {
{% for svc in xaisen_public_services %}
{% set xaisen_prefix = '/' ~ xaisen_provider ~ '-' ~ xaisen_host ~ '/' ~ svc.service ~ '-' ~ svc.index %}
    @{{ svc.service }}{{ svc.index }} path {{ xaisen_prefix }} {{ xaisen_prefix }}/*
    handle @{{ svc.service }}{{ svc.index }} {
        uri strip_prefix {{ xaisen_prefix }}
{% if svc.service in xaisen_metrics_ports %}
        handle /metrics* {
            reverse_proxy xaisen-{{ svc.worker_key | default(svc.service) }}:{{ xaisen_metrics_ports[svc.service] }}
        }
{% if svc.service in xaisen_healthz_ports %}
        handle /healthz* {
            reverse_proxy xaisen-{{ svc.worker_key | default(svc.service) }}:{{ xaisen_healthz_ports[svc.service] }}
        }
{% endif %}
{% if svc.service in xaisen_turn_rpc_ports %}
        handle /turn/* {
            reverse_proxy xaisen-{{ svc.worker_key | default(svc.service) }}:{{ xaisen_turn_rpc_ports[svc.service] }}
        }
{% endif %}
{% if svc.service in xaisen_stats_report_services %}
        handle /stats/* {
            reverse_proxy xaisen-{{ svc.worker_key | default(svc.service) }}:{{ xaisen_metrics_ports[svc.service] }}
        }
        handle /logs/* {
            reverse_proxy xaisen-{{ svc.worker_key | default(svc.service) }}:{{ xaisen_metrics_ports[svc.service] }}
        }
        handle /relay-down-hint* {
            reverse_proxy xaisen-{{ svc.worker_key | default(svc.service) }}:{{ xaisen_metrics_ports[svc.service] }}
        }
{% endif %}
{% if svc.service in xaisen_internal_ports %}
        handle {
            reverse_proxy xaisen-{{ svc.worker_key | default(svc.service) }}:{{ xaisen_internal_ports[svc.service] }}
        }
{% endif %}
{% else %}
        reverse_proxy xaisen-{{ svc.worker_key | default(svc.service) }}:{{ xaisen_internal_ports[svc.service] }}
{% endif %}
    }
{% endfor %}
}
